# AY <-> VeriPB checker PIN. Single source of truth.
#
# Everything that verifies an AY proof — CI, `scripts/ci/pb_certified_gate.sh`,
# and the Rust gate in `crates/ay-test-support/src/veripb.rs` (module `pin`,
# which `include_str!`s THIS file) — reads the checker identity from here.
# There is no second copy to drift.
#
# WHY A PIN AT ALL. "VeriPB accepted it" only means something if you can say
# WHICH VeriPB. Upstream 3.0.2 as published has TWENTY-ONE confirmed wrong-verdict
# defects (see ci/veripb-soundness/README.md): it will print `s VERIFIED
# UNSATISFIABLE` for satisfiable formulas and `s VERIFIED SATISFIABLE` for
# unsatisfiable ones, given the right (small, non-adversarial-looking) proof.
# A green CI run against such a checker is not evidence. So the pin names an
# exact commit PLUS the patches that fix all twenty-one, and the gate re-proves
# the fixes on every run against committed fixtures before it trusts a verdict.
#
# HOW BAD IT GETS. Four of them need NOTHING from the formula, and two of those
# four contain no arithmetic anywhere. Defect 14 (order auxiliary variables are
# not order-private) forges `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` for an ARBITRARY
# satisfiable formula in four proof lines, by asserting an order's private
# variable TRUE at the top level and then letting `scope` inject the order's own
# specification — which says the opposite — as unconditional premises. Defect 15
# (the legacy version 2.0 parser enforces no rule scope) does the mirror image in
# six lines: `sol` inside an unterminated `pre_order` is checked against the
# ORDER's private empty database, so it "finds" a solution to a formula that has
# none and `conclusion SAT` publishes it. Defect 10
# (propagation slack computed in the row's own width) forges
# `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` against an ordinary satisfiable clause in three
# proof lines. Defect 11 (a proof-by-contradiction subproof FABRICATING a
# database proofgoal) does it in six, with no arithmetic anywhere and no extreme
# number in sight — and through checked deletion the same fabrication deletes a
# core constraint for free, forging `s VERIFIED SATISFIABLE` for an
# unsatisfiable formula. While either was open, no verdict from an unpatched
# 3.0.2 was evidence of anything for any formula. That is the standard this pin
# exists to hold: not "the checker is usually right", but "these exact wrong
# verdicts are re-proved impossible on every run".
#
# TWENTY-ONE DEFECTS, TWENTY-TWO FIXTURES. Keep both numbers in view; they are
# different things and conflating them is what made an earlier revision of this
# file say "six" when the audit said "eight". The defect count is TWENTY-ONE. The
# fixture count is TWENTY-TWO, because defect 7 (normalization wrapping) has two
# manifestations with OPPOSITE wrong verdicts and each needs its own pair.
# Defects 5 and 7 were found late, during the other fixes, and shipped with
# in-tree Rust regression tests but no standalone fixture; fixtures 07/08/09
# close that gap. Defects 9 (`pol` addition wrapping the cancellation
# subtraction) and 10 (propagation slack wrapping) were found later still, by
# re-auditing the code the first patch did NOT touch for the classes it did
# fix; fixtures 10 and 11 cover them. Defects 11 to 13 came from auditing the
# SUBPROOF, SUBSTITUTION and ORDER machinery, which neither earlier pass had
# looked at, and two of the three are not arithmetic bugs at all. Defects 14 to
# 21 came from auditing the surfaces that decide what a verdict MEANS — order
# variable scoping, the legacy version 2.0 parser, solution logging, the
# conclusion and output guarantees, and the WCNF front end — and NONE of the
# eight is an arithmetic bug. Two of them need nothing from the formula at all.
# The mapping is total:
#   1->01  2->02  3->03  4->04  5->09  6->05  7->07 and 08  8->06  9->10
#   10->11  11->12  12->13  13->14  14->15  15->16  16->17  17->18  18->19
#   19->20  20->21  21->22
#
# FORMAT: strict KEY=VALUE, no spaces, no quoting, no expansion. It is `.`-able
# from POSIX sh and parsed by a hand-written Rust reader; keep it boring.
#
# TO MOVE THE PIN (checker upgrade):
#   1. Rebase BOTH patches onto the new upstream commit, in order.
#   2. Update VERIPB_COMMIT / VERIPB_VERSION / VERIPB_PATCH_SHA256 /
#      VERIPB_PATCH2_SHA256 here.
#   3. Run `scripts/ci/pb_certified_gate.sh`. It rebuilds from scratch, re-runs
#      the twenty-two soundness fixtures, and re-verifies every certified-track
#      proof. If the new checker regressed any of the twenty-two, the gate fails
#      and the upgrade does not land. That is the whole point: a checker swap
#      cannot change what "verified" means without turning CI red.

# Upstream repository the pinned checker is built from.
VERIPB_REPO=https://gitlab.com/MIAOresearch/software/VeriPB.git

# Exact upstream commit. `--version` is NOT an identity: 3.0.2 has been many
# different checkers. The sha is the identity; the version is a cross-check.
VERIPB_COMMIT=4bb10c2ce98a8c80122a7a2399d7f35ffdc6e58e

# What the pinned build must report for `veripb --version` (last field).
VERIPB_VERSION=3.0.2

# TWO PATCHES, APPLIED IN ORDER. VERIPB_PATCH is the byte-verified fork delta
# (see below) and VERIPB_PATCH2 is a fix this repository wrote itself. They are
# separate files ON PURPOSE: VERIPB_PATCH's whole claim to trust is that it is
# `git diff 4bb10c2c f7f9d442` of the private fork VERBATIM, checkable with
# `cmp` against a fresh clone. Folding a local fix into it would destroy that
# property and there would be no way to tell the two apart again. So local work
# lands as a NEW numbered patch and the fork stays the authority for 0001.
#
# THE FORK IS NOW BEHIND THIS REPO. `alab-main` carries none of the fixes for
# defects 9 to 21; ci/veripb-patches/0002-*.patch is the delta that closes the
# gap. Push it to the fork (as commits on `alab-main`) at the next opportunity,
# then re-cut VERIPB_PATCH from the new fork tip and drop 0002 in the SAME
# change, so this file never claims a provenance the fork cannot back. Nothing
# here pushes to the fork; it is external.
#
# 0002 is the one file this repository may EDIT IN PLACE: it has no external
# byte-provenance to preserve, so a further locally found defect extends it
# (and its recorded sha256) rather than adding a third numbered patch. 0001 is
# the opposite — never edit it, see below.
#
# Patch applied on top of VERIPB_COMMIT before building. It is the FOUR-commit
# delta of the private fork branch `alab-main` ENDING AT f7f9d442 — NOT the
# branch tip. `git merge-base main alab-main` = VERIPB_COMMIT, so the patch is
# `git diff 4bb10c2c..f7f9d442` verbatim:
#
#   a7836ca6  fix(diagnostics): empty propagation trails; stop double-reporting
#   ef29860b  fix(soundness): the EIGHT wrong-verdict defects, plus robustness
#   9bcfba52  feat(proved-core): the Clean-proved kernel (veripb-core-proved)
#   f7f9d442  fix(proved-core): the kernel now checks 105/105
#
# 68 files, +5714/-95. Reproducible from public sources: clone + checkout +
# `git apply` + `cargo build --release --bin veripb`. Verified 2026-08-24 from
# a fresh clone of the vendored veripb fork: `git diff 4bb10c2c f7f9d442`
# is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the committed patch file (sha256 bcb78b38..., `cmp` clean).
#
# WHY THE PIN IS NOT AT THE BRANCH TIP. As of 2026-08-24 alab-main is 34 ahead
# of VERIPB_COMMIT (0 behind); f7f9d442 is the 4th of those 34. Moving the pin
# to the tip would take the patch from 68 files/+5714 to 305 files/+37673, and
# the tip's proved kernel DOES NOT CHECK on the only Clean binary present here:
#
#   clean 1.2.0, from the `trust` stage3-tools-bin tree (on the box where this
#   was measured, .../trust/build/aarch64-apple-darwin/stage3-tools-bin/
#   aarch64-apple-darwin/clean)
#     f7f9d442  kernel: 105 decls, 105 passed,   0 failed   (rc 0)
#     tip       kernel: 417 decls, 341 passed,  76 failed   (rc 1)
#
# The 76 are not rotted proofs: 67 are cascading `Unknown identifier` from 9
# roots, and the roots are TOOLCHAIN GAPS, not syntax. `Nat.eq_of_beq_eq_true`
# (4 sites) does not exist in clean 1.2.0 under that or any probed alias, and
# `BitVec` (5 sites, the i64 machine-width capcoeff contracts) does not exist
# at all — it auto-binds as a universe parameter. The fork's own pins name a
# DIFFERENT Clean lineage, `clean 0.1.0|v0.1.0-22-g6095aa72e` built out of a
# `clean` checkout's own target/user/debug, which is not present on the box
# this was measured on. Until that toolchain is available, the tip is
# UNVERIFIABLE here and the
# pin stays at f7f9d442, the last commit whose kernel checks green.
#
# NOTE ON THE PROVED KERNEL. `veripb-core-proved` is a workspace MEMBER that
# nothing depends on: `cargo build --bin veripb` does not compile it, and the
# shipped binary contains none of its symbols. It is built and tested by the
# gate (phase 2b) so it cannot rot, but it does not decide any verdict. Do not
# read its presence in this patch as an increase in the trustworthiness of any
# existing certification claim. See ci/veripb-soundness/README.md.
VERIPB_PATCH=ci/veripb-patches/0001-ay-validated-soundness-fixes.patch
VERIPB_PATCH_SHA256=bcb78b38893cc565d716b33dc79a9140d7a119fd9eb90f77d423cced5903e5c7

# SECOND patch, applied on top of VERIPB_PATCH. Written HERE, not in the fork.
# It fixes the THIRTEEN wrong-verdict defects 0001 does not. Defects 9 and 10
# came from re-auditing the code 0001 did not touch for the classes it did fix;
# defects 11 to 13 came from auditing the SUBPROOF, SUBSTITUTION and ORDER
# machinery, which neither earlier pass had looked at; defects 14 to 21 came
# from auditing the surfaces that decide what a verdict MEANS rather than how a
# number is computed — variable SCOPING, the legacy version 2.0 PARSER, solution
# LOGGING, the conclusion and output GUARANTEES, and the WCNF front end. Ten of
# these thirteen are not arithmetic bugs at all, which is the point: an audit
# that only looks for the last defect class finds only the last defect class.
#
#   DEFECT 9 (fixture 10) — `pol`'s `+` range-checks the SUM of the two degrees
#   and then subtracts the literal-cancellation constant in the narrow integer
#   type, unchecked. That constant is always >= 0, so the subtraction can only
#   UNDERFLOW, and with overflow checks off in release a row that is MORE
#   trivially true than its operands is stored with `degree > coeff_sum` — the
#   empty contradiction. On the 0001-only build the formula `1 x1 1 x2 >= 1 ;`
#   (the clause x1 OR x2, satisfied by x1=1) plus a six-line proof yields
#   `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE`, and the same wrapped row forges
#   `s VERIFIED BOUNDS INF <= obj <= INF`. Present in all four `add`
#   implementations (`Clause`, `Cardinality`, `GeneralPBConstraint<i64>`,
#   `GeneralPBConstraint<i128>`), because widening moves the threshold rather
#   than closing the hole.
#
#   DEFECT 10 (fixture 11) — the propagator computes its slack
#   `coeff_sum - degree` in the row's own width, at three sites in
#   `GeneralPBWatcher`. Both operands fit; their DIFFERENCE need not. A trivially
#   true row whose slack exceeds the type wraps NEGATIVE, so every coefficient
#   compares as greater than the slack and the row is reported to propagate ALL
#   its literals unconditionally. Two such rows (over `x1` and over `~x1`), both
#   accepted legitimately as trivially true RUP conclusions, leave the trail in
#   conflict, and `rup >= 1 ;` then derives the empty contradiction. The poison
#   is entirely in the PROOF, so at a 0001-only pin no
#   `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` was evidence of anything for any formula, and
#   every RUP-backed auto-discharge was free.
#
#   DEFECT 11 (fixture 12) — `SubproofContext::pop_database_goal` FABRICATES a proofgoal
#   `C_id | witness` when the named database ID has no registered one, instead of
#   erroring the way `pop_internal_goal` always has. A proof-by-contradiction
#   subproof registers NO database goals (`pbc` adds none; the version 2.0
#   `red`/`delc` spellings that map to one have an empty witness, which touches
#   nothing), so every database ID lands in that branch and the fabricated goal
#   is `C_id` itself — whose scope premise `¬C_id` contradicts the still present
#   `C_id` at once. `end_proofgoal` then set `contradiction_proven` on the
#   ENCLOSING subproof and `finalize` short-circuited, so the obligation a `pbc`
#   owes — a contradiction from `¬C` — was never checked. Six proof lines, NO
#   ARITHMETIC, forge `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` for any formula; through `delc`
#   under `strengthening_to_core` the same fabrication deletes a CORE constraint
#   for free and forges `s VERIFIED SATISFIABLE` for an unsatisfiable one. The
#   fix keeps the one legitimate promotion (goal `#1` of an empty-witness
#   `red`/`delc`, whose premise IS the subproof's `¬C`) and refuses the rest.
#
#   DEFECT 12 (fixture 13) — `substitute` lowers the degree in the row's own width:
#   `GeneralPBConstraint` does `substituted_degree -= &term.coeff` and
#   `Cardinality` does `substituted_degree -= 1`, unchecked, per literal mapped
#   to the constant TRUE. The trivially true `1 x2 >= i64::MIN` under `x2 -> 1`
#   has the true degree `i64::MIN - 1` and was stored as `>= i64::MAX` over an
#   EMPTY left-hand side — the empty contradiction. `implies()` short-circuits on
#   a contradicting source (correctly, for a genuine one), so with such a row in
#   the database `check_substituted_implication` reported "implied" for every
#   target and `Proofgoal::autoprove` discharged ANY goal of a `red` step as
#   `DBImpliesSubstituted`. Five proof lines forge `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` for
#   a satisfiable formula, and the wrap also reaches the order-SPECIFICATION
#   surface with no `rup` line at all. Present in `Cardinality`,
#   `GeneralPBConstraint<i64>` and `GeneralPBConstraint<i128>` — widening moves
#   the threshold rather than closing the hole.
#
#   DEFECT 13 (fixture 14) — `def_order` never validates the `fresh_right` / `fresh_aux_1` /
#   `fresh_aux_2` lists of its transitivity proof.
#   `OrderFreshRightVariablesRule::compute` stored them verbatim and
#   `get_transitivity_substitution` zipped them against the order's variables
#   with `izip!`, which STOPS AT THE SHORTEST iterator. `fresh_right ;` is legal
#   (the list is optional per the development design notes) and yields the empty list, so
#   both substitutions came out EMPTY and every transitivity proofgoal
#   `D_i|x→z` was literally `D_i` — already a premise — which an empty
#   `proof … qed;` discharged by database lookup. Declaring the RIGHT variables
#   again as `fresh_right` has the correct arity and does the same thing through
#   the identity substitution. Either way a relation whose strict part contains a
#   CYCLE installs as a preorder, `dom`'s descent argument becomes vacuous, and a
#   four-line bogus order forges `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` for a satisfiable
#   formula.
#
#   DEFECT 14 (fixture 15) — order AUXILIARY VARIABLES are not order-private.
#   They live in the global variable namespace, and `validate_aux_var` admitted
#   them exactly where it must not: with no order loaded its `is_none_or`
#   admitted EVERY variable, and with one loaded it admitted precisely that
#   order's auxiliary variables. So a top-level `red +1 $a1 >= 1 : $a1 -> 1;`
#   asserts one TRUE for free — its own proofgoal is trivial under the witness.
#   The order's SPECIFICATION says `~$a1`, and `scope leq`/`scope geq` injects
#   that specification as unconditional premises inside any `dom` or `red`
#   subproof, so the database is contradictory inside every scope proofgoal, one
#   `rup >= 1;` discharges them all, and `qed` publishes the empty contradiction
#   at top level. FOUR proof lines forge `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` for an
#   ARBITRARY satisfiable formula; three reach paths (via `dom`, via `red`, and
#   with the variable named BEFORE `load_order`, where the check was a total
#   no-op) and a fourth in version 2.0, where auxiliary variables are spelled as
#   ordinary identifiers and the parse-time check never runs at all.
#
#   DEFECT 15 (fixture 16) — the legacy version 2.0 parser has NO rule-scope
#   enforcement. Its version 3.0 sibling has always rejected `The rule X is not
#   allowed in a Y context`; `grep -n scope` in the version 2.0 parser returned
#   nothing. So `sol` inside an unterminated `pre_order` validates against the
#   ORDER's private EMPTY scratch database — `check_solution` finds nothing to
#   falsify — sets `best_valid_objective_value`, and the hint-less
#   `conclusion SAT`, for which that value is the sole evidence, publishes
#   `s VERIFIED SATISFIABLE` for a formula with ZERO models. Six lines, any
#   unsatisfiable formula, no arithmetic.
#
#   DEFECT 16 (fixture 17) — `conclusion ENUMERATION_COMPLETE n` accepts ANY
#   database contradiction with no provenance test, while counting only `solx`
#   exclusions. An `obji`-installed objective-improving constraint removes every
#   solution that is not strictly better than a chosen value and can make the
#   database contradictory by itself, so `obji 1; rup >= 1;
#   conclusion ENUMERATION_COMPLETE 0;` certifies "0 solutions" for a formula
#   with one, in six lines. `soli` reaches it in five.
#
#   DEFECT 17 (fixture 18) — `solx` with a legal but EMPTY `preserved:` set
#   builds its exclusion over no literals: the empty `>= 1`, i.e. the empty
#   CONTRADICTION, installed in the database AND the core. Two proof lines then
#   forge an arbitrary optimality bound for a satisfiable formula and make it
#   "equisatisfiable" to anything at all.
#
#   DEFECT 18 (fixture 19) — `conclusion BOUNDS <lb>` short-circuits: any lower
#   bound not better than the best logged objective is granted the moment the
#   database contains ANY contradiction — including one assembled out of the
#   `solx` exclusions themselves, which remove solutions rather than following
#   from the problem. Four `solx` lines and a `rup` forge
#   `s VERIFIED BOUNDS 10 <= obj <= 10` for a problem whose optimum is 0.
#   Deleting the short-circuit does not fix it: the fallback implication check
#   also succeeds by propagation against a contradicting database, so the
#   exclusions are what must not be live.
#
#   DEFECT 19 (fixture 20) — `output EQUISATISFIABLE` and `output EQUIOPTIMAL`
#   are not gated against exported solution exclusions; only `EQUIENUMERABLE`
#   was. Three `solx` lines export the exclusions into the output formula and
#   certify a 3-solution formula EQUISATISFIABLE to an unsatisfiable one.
#
#   DEFECT 20 (fixture 21) — plain `sol` does not set `solution_logged`, so
#   `sol …; obji v;` evades defect 19's guard while the semantically identical
#   single rule `soli … : v;` is caught. A textbook asymmetric-sibling bug: the
#   guard tested a proxy ("a solution was logged") instead of the hazard ("a
#   solution-restricting constraint is live"), and the proxy was wrong in both
#   directions — it also stayed set after the constraint had been deleted again.
#
#   DEFECT 21 (fixture 22) — the WCNF parser names variables from the literal
#   TOKEN TEXT instead of its VALUE, building `"x" + &lex.slice()[1..]`. The
#   DIMACS spellings `-1`, `-01` and `-0001` are one literal; the parser made
#   them two or three VARIABLES. The infeasible MaxSAT instance `h 1 0` /
#   `h -01 0` parses as the satisfiable `(x1) AND (~x01)` and is certified as
#   having an optimal solution. The sibling CNF and OPB parsers were audited for
#   the same mistake and do not have it.
#
# The arithmetic fixes (9, 10, 12) use the idiom 0001 already uses
# (`finish_weakening`, `lower_rhs`, `normalization_fits_in_type`): checked
# machine arithmetic on the hot path, and escalation to BigInt — never a clamp —
# when a value leaves the type, so what the checker stores and compares is the
# TRUE value. The other ten are not arithmetic: 11, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19
# and 20 are missing or misplaced checks, fixed by adding the check and failing
# closed, and 21 is a parser reading a token's text where it needed its value.
# Fixtures 10 to 22, their `variants/`, and their opposite-verdict companions
# are the gated evidence; the patch also carries in-tree regression tests, which
# fail on a 0001-only tree — including POSITIVE ones, because eight of these
# thirteen fixes RESTRICT a rule and a restriction that goes one step too far is
# its own defect. Two of the positives are upstream's OWN tests
# (`dominance_with_aux_vars`, which uses auxiliary variables inside a `scope`,
# and `optimization_output_opposing_lits_objective`, a legitimate `soli` +
# `EQUIOPTIMAL` proof); both would fail under the obvious over-broad fix and
# both are why defects 14 and 19/20 are fixed the way they are.
#
# 115 files, +2560/-126. Reproducible the same way as 0001: clone + checkout +
# `git apply` 0001 + `git apply` 0002 + `cargo build --release --bin veripb`.
VERIPB_PATCH2=ci/veripb-patches/0002-ay-post-fork-soundness-fixes.patch
VERIPB_PATCH2_SHA256=fad6c2bc60a3a8a65a2af71a23a67e88692cd5b324e6996a4405b2a204220bf7

# Fixtures the pinned checker MUST reject (one directory per known bug).
VERIPB_SOUNDNESS_DIR=ci/veripb-soundness

# The certified-track instance set and its expected verdicts.
VERIPB_CERT_MANIFEST=ci/cert-instances/manifest.tsv
